  'hero.kicker': 'ECONOMICS · MACRO MEASUREMENT',
  'hero.title': 'How an Economy Is Measured',
  'hero.sub': 'How do we measure how well an economy is doing?',
  'hero.lead': 'Every day the news is filled with figures: how much GDP grew, how fast CPI rose, whether unemployment is high or low, and whether total factor productivity is accelerating or stalling. Yet there is no natural, ready-made ruler for "how much a country actually produced in a year." Every macroeconomic metric is a deliberate choice among boundaries, weights, and accounting definitions. This essay unpacks ten core indicators, inviting you to interactively explore the underlying equations and historical data to see what each metric captures—and what it deliberately or unavoidably leaves out.',
  'hero.note': '<strong>Author & Model Note:</strong> This essay was authored by AI and verified by unit tests (see BRIEF.md for model and data details). The mini-economies in Chapters 1–9 are simplified didactic models designed to make accounting identities and index biases tangible; empirical macro and revision vintages in Chapters 4, 6, 7, 8, and 10 come from public FRED, ALFRED, and Penn World Table (PWT) data (retrieved 2026-11-13), with latest country figures from IMF, NBS, and Eurostat releases. This article explains statistical mechanisms and known limitations; it makes no economic forecasts and constitutes neither investment nor policy advice.',

