  /* ── ch1: GDP three ways ── */
  'ch1.title': 'Why a Loaf of Bread Gets Counted Three Times from Three Angles',
  'ch1.law': 'Gross Domestic Product (GDP) counts only newly added value. Calculating it via production, expenditure, or income must yield the exact same total; including intermediate goods would double-count output out of thin air.',
  'ch1.p1': 'Imagine a mini-economy with just four entities: a farm sells wheat to a flour mill for <strong>30</strong>, the mill grinds it into flour and sells it to a bakery for <strong>55</strong>, the bakery bakes bread and sells it to consumers for <strong>100</strong>, and an equipment maker sells the bakery a new oven for <strong>20</strong>. Summing every transaction produces total gross sales (gross output) of <strong>205</strong>—yet that clearly overstates reality, because the wheat was counted three times and the flour twice.',
  'ch1.p2': 'To eliminate double counting, national accounting relies on <strong>value added</strong>: each stage records only "revenue from sales minus purchases of raw materials." This accounting rule binds three entirely different ledgers to the exact same total—under a standard simplified closed economy without government:',
  'ch1.eq': 'GDP ＝ <i>Σ</i>(Output − Intermediate Inputs) ＝ <i>C</i> + <i>I</i> + <i>NX</i> ＝ Wages + Profits (including rents and indirect taxes)',
  'ch1.model.title': 'Model 1 · Three Approaches to GDP in a Mini-Economy',
  'ch1.model.how': 'Drag the sliders for wheat price, imported cheese, and outsourced baking. Watch how gross sales, all three GDP approaches, and "beyond-boundary" home production move in lockstep.',
  'ch1.model.aria': 'Comparison bar chart of GDP production, expenditure, and income approaches vs outside-boundary household production',
