  'ch1.say.base': 'Default state: Gross sales of <em>{gross}</em> contain <em>{dup}</em> of double counting; removing intermediate inputs leaves production, expenditure, and income all exactly equal to <em>{gdp}</em> (before deducting depreciation of <em>{depr}</em>, yielding Net Domestic Product NDP = <em>{ndp}</em>).',
  'ch1.say.wheat': 'When wheat rises to <em>{wheat}</em> while bread remains 100, the mill’s value added is squeezed to <em>{mill}</em> and gross sales rise to <em>{gross}</em>, yet all three GDP measures remain rock-solid at <em>{gdp}</em>.',
  'ch1.say.import': 'Consumers buy <em>{imp}</em> of imported cheese: consumption <i>C</i> rises to <em>{C}</em> while net exports <i>NX</i> drops to <em>{nx}</em>, exactly cancelling out so domestic GDP remains <em>{gdp}</em>.',
  'ch1.say.home': 'Shifting <em>{shift}</em> of previously home-baked bread to commercial purchases: total bread consumed across society is unchanged (still <em>{tot}</em>), yet GDP jumps from 120 to <em>{gdp}</em>!',
  'ch1.p3': 'Try raising the wheat price from 30 to 40: gross sales jump to <strong>215</strong>, farm value added expands to 40, and the flour mill shrinks to 15, yet all three GDP measures remain unchanged at <strong>120</strong>. Next, slide imported cheese to 25: many believe that "subtracting imports in the expenditure identity (<i>NX</i> = <i>X</i> − <i>M</i>) means imports drag down GDP." In reality, imported goods are not produced domestically at all; they are first captured in consumption <i>C</i> (which rises to 125) and then subtracted in <i>NX</i> (becoming <strong>−25</strong>) purely to cancel out foreign production, leaving domestic GDP unchanged at <strong>120</strong>.',
  'ch1.p4': 'The most revealing slider is the third: households initially bake 30 worth of bread at home. Under the <strong>production boundary</strong> of national accounting, unpaid domestic work is excluded from GDP. Once 20 of that baking is outsourced to a store, society consumes the exact same <strong>130</strong> units of bread, yet GDP leaps from 120 to <strong>140</strong>. Second-hand home and car sales (brokerage commissions aside), pure transfer payments (such as state pensions), and illicit informal transactions are likewise excluded. Furthermore, the "Gross" in GDP signifies that annual fixed capital depreciation of <strong>12</strong> is not deducted—subtracting it yields Net Domestic Product (NDP = <strong>108</strong>).',
  'ch1.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> This model omits government purchases, indirect taxes, and inventory adjustments, and fixes wages at 65% of value added; real-world national accounts (such as SNA) also impute services for owner-occupied housing and non-market government services.',

  /* ── ch2: Nominal vs Real & Price Indexes ── */
  'ch2.title': 'Nominal vs Real: Price Indexes Are a Choice',
  'ch2.law': 'Decomposing "spending more money this year" into "buying more goods" versus "prices going up" entirely depends on which year’s basket of goods you use as your weights.',
  'ch2.p1': 'Last year computing power cost 100 per unit; this year the price plunged 25% to 75, so people bought vastly more of it. Meanwhile haircuts and rent rose from 100 to 106, so people trimmed their consumption slightly. Dividing nominal GDP by a price deflator yields real GDP—but should you weight prices by the initial basket of quantities, or by the ending basket?',
  'ch2.p2': 'Statistical agencies face three classic yardsticks: the <strong>Laspeyres index</strong> clings to the base-period basket <i>q</i><sub>0</sub>, ignoring how consumers substitute toward cheaper computing power; the <strong>Paasche index</strong> looks solely at the current basket <i>q<sub>t</sub></i>; and the <strong>Fisher ideal index</strong> takes their geometric mean. Multiplying successive adjacent annual Fisher changes yields the <strong>chained Fisher index</strong>:',
  'ch2.eq': '<i>P<sub>L</sub></i> = Σ<i>p<sub>t</sub>q</i><sub>0</sub> / Σ<i>p</i><sub>0</sub><i>q</i><sub>0</sub> ,&nbsp; <i>P<sub>P</sub></i> = Σ<i>p<sub>t</sub>q<sub>t</sub></i> / Σ<i>p</i><sub>0</sub><i>q<sub>t</sub></i> ,&nbsp; <i>P<sub>F</sub></i> = √(<i>P<sub>L</sub></i> · <i>P<sub>P</sub></i>)',
  'ch2.model.title': 'Model 2 · Price Indexes and Substitution Bias Lab',
  'ch2.model.how': 'Adjust the annual price drop of computing, the elasticity of substitution <i>σ</i> (consumer price sensitivity), and import share. The top chart shows four price index trajectories; the bottom shows real GDP growth over two years under each index.',
  'ch2.model.aria': 'Charts of Laspeyres, Paasche, chained Fisher, and true utility price indexes from Year 0 to Year 2 alongside real GDP growth bars',
  'ch2.leg.l': 'Laspeyres (Fixed Base Basket)',
  'ch2.leg.p': 'Paasche (Current Basket)',
