  'ch2.cv.tru': 'True Utility',
  'ch2.say.base': 'Over two years nominal GDP changed by <em>{nomG}%</em>. The fixed-base Laspeyres index claims prices rose to <em>{pL}</em>, putting real growth at <em>{realL}%</em>; chained Fisher deflates prices to <em>{pC}</em>, showing real GDP changed by <em>{realF}%</em> (closely tracking the true utility growth of 6.1%).',
  'ch2.say.nosub': 'When substitution elasticity <i>σ</i> = 0 (consumers do not buy more computing despite price cuts), Laspeyres, Paasche, and Fisher collapse identically to <em>{pL}</em>, and substitution bias is <em>0.0</em> pts.',
  'ch2.say.import': 'If <em>{imp}%</em> of discounted tech goods are imported: they remain fully reflected in household CPI (<em>{cpi}</em>), but are purged from the domestic GDP deflator (<em>{def}</em>), causing the two to diverge.',
  'ch2.p3': 'Under default parameters (computing prices dropping <strong>25%</strong> annually, services rising 6%, elasticity of substitution <strong>1.5</strong>), two-year nominal GDP increases by just <strong>1.7%</strong>. Fixed to Year 0 weights, Laspeyres fixates on services having an 80% initial budget share, reporting prices rose to <strong>101.1</strong> and deflating real growth to a meager <strong>0.6%</strong>. In contrast, current-weighted Paasche drops to <strong>89.1</strong> and chained Fisher lands at <strong>95.6</strong>, uncovering robust real growth of <strong>6.3%</strong>. The <strong>6.2</strong> index-point gap between them is <strong>substitution bias</strong>. Dragging <i>σ</i> to 0 collapses all three indexes back into a single point (<strong>101.1</strong>).',
  'ch2.p4': 'In the real world, the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) suffered from fixed-base Laspeyres distortions during the 1990s IT revolution when computer prices plummeted and unit sales surged, prompting its official switch in <strong>January 1996</strong> to chained Fisher indexes for real GDP. It is equally essential to distinguish <strong>CPI (Consumer Price Index)</strong> from the <strong>GDP deflator</strong>: CPI tracks a basket of consumer purchases (including imports, excluding industrial machinery), whereas the GDP deflator tracks everything produced domestically (including investment goods and exports, excluding imports). Increasing the "Tech Import Share" slider clearly shows them parting ways.',
  'ch2.see': 'Further reading: On the monetary forces and nominal anchors behind inflation, see <a href="../../money/where-money-comes-from/#ch4">Chapter 4 of “Where Money Comes From”</a>.',
  'ch2.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> The two-good CES model features only smooth continuous substitution, omitting discrete arrivals of entirely new goods or qualitative leaps at unchanged sticker prices—the hardest real-world CPI challenges explored in the next chapter.',

  /* ── ch3: CPI in practice ── */
  'ch3.title': 'CPI in Practice: Why Your Shopping Cart Disagrees with Official Stats',
  'ch3.law': 'Official CPI reflects aggregate societal expenditure shares and treats quality improvements as implicit price cuts; if your spending habits or valuation of quality differ, your experienced inflation will diverge.',
  'ch3.p1': 'Why does the news report inflation at 2%–3% while rent and gas feel suffocating? The first reason is <strong>expenditure weighting</strong>. Standard CPI baskets aggregate total dollars spent across society, not the average budget of a median household. The second reason is <strong>Owners’ Equivalent Rent (OER)</strong>: homeowners do not pay monthly rent. To avoid mixing capital asset investment with consumption, statistical bureaus estimate what an owner would pay to rent their own home. In the U.S. CPI, shelter (dominated by OER) commands about a third of the entire basket.',
  'ch3.p2': 'The third reason is <strong>hedonic quality adjustment</strong>. A laptop sold for $1,500 last year; this year an updated model with double the memory and speed still sells for $1,500. Although its sticker price never dropped, statisticians count the doubled capability as a steep quality-adjusted price reduction. In 1996, the landmark <strong>Boskin Commission</strong> reported to the U.S. Senate that CPI overstated changes in the true cost of living by about <strong>1.10</strong> percentage points annually (upper/lower-level substitution accounted for 0.15 + 0.25 pp, outlet bias 0.10 pp, and quality change/new products a dominant <strong>0.60</strong> pp). The Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) subsequently adopted geometric weighting and expanded hedonic modeling; subsequent studies (e.g. Lebow & Rudd 2003) estimated the remaining bias narrowed, yet remained around <strong>0.9</strong> percentage points.',
  'ch3.model.title': 'Model 3 · Personal Basket & CPI Deconstruction Lab',
  'ch3.model.how': 'Adjust your shelter share, energy share, and energy price shock, then toggle quality adjustment and Owners’ Equivalent Rent (OER) to compare four distinct CPI inflation metrics.',
  'ch3.model.aria': 'Comparison chart of headline CPI, core CPI, personal experienced CPI, and unadjusted CPI',
