  'ch3.say.base': 'Current headline CPI is <em>{head}%</em>, while core CPI (excluding food and energy) is <em>{core}%</em>; your basket matches official weights, leaving a gap of <em>{diff}</em> pp.',
  'ch3.say.diff': 'At identical prices, your personal CPI is <em>{pers}%</em>, which is <em>{absdiff}</em> percentage points {dir} than official headline CPI (<em>{head}%</em>)!',
  'ch3.say.nohed': 'Disabling hedonic quality adjustments immediately adds <em>{hdiff}</em> percentage points to headline CPI (raising it to <em>{head}%</em>)—the implicit deflationary dividend of doubled electronics specs evaporates.',
  'ch3.dir.high': 'higher',
  'ch3.dir.low': 'lower',
  'ch3.p3': 'Set the energy shock to +30%, shelter weight to <strong>45%</strong>, and energy to <strong>15%</strong> (typical for a long-distance commuter renting an apartment): official core CPI remains frozen at <strong>1.44%</strong> and headline CPI reads <strong>3.66%</strong>, while your personal inflation surges to <strong>6.58%</strong>—a full <strong>2.92</strong> percentage points higher. Conversely, switching off hedonic adjustments (assuming tech hardware upgrades are not treated as price declines) lifts baseline headline CPI by <strong>1.56</strong> percentage points from <strong>2.40%</strong> to <strong>3.96%</strong>; removing OER to leave only tenant rent trims headline CPI by <strong>0.48</strong> percentage points down to <strong>1.92%</strong>.',
  'ch3.p4': 'This explains why central banks focus so intensely on <strong>core inflation (Core CPI / Core PCE)</strong>: not because policymakers think families do not buy groceries or gasoline, but because volatile energy and agricultural swings revert quickly to the mean, masking persistent underlying trends. As of <strong>October 2026</strong>, U.S. headline CPI was <strong>4.08%</strong> y/y and core CPI was <strong>2.87%</strong>; as of <strong>September 2026</strong>, the Federal Reserve’s preferred Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) price index rose <strong>3.65%</strong> headline and <strong>3.12%</strong> core—PCE weights update continuously to reflect consumer substitution like a Fisher chain, whereas CPI weights update far more sluggishly.',
  'ch3.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> The five expenditure weights and baseline inflation figures here are didactic parameters demonstrating sensitivity to definitions; treatment of owner-occupied housing varies across nations (e.g. the Eurozone HICP historically excludes OER entirely).',

  /* ── ch4: Growth-rate traps ── */
  'ch4.title': 'Four Traps in Growth Rates: Annualisation, Base Effects, Seasons & Revisions',
  'ch4.law': 'Before interpreting a growth headline, ask four questions: Is it quarterly annualized or year-on-year? Did the comparison quarter last year suffer a collapse? Have seasonal swings been stripped out? And how much will this vintage be revised?',
  'ch4.p1': 'One headline reports "Q2 GDP grew 3.4% q/q SAAR," while another notes "Q2 GDP rose 2.1% y/y." The <strong>quarter-on-quarter seasonally adjusted annualized rate (q/q SAAR)</strong> compounds a single quarter’s pace over four quarters to capture immediate momentum; <strong>year-on-year growth (y/y)</strong> compares this quarter directly to the same quarter last year, embedding the collective memory of the past four quarters:',
  'ch4.eq': 'q/q SAAR = (1 + <i>g<sub>q</sub></i>)<sup>4</sup> − 1 ,&nbsp;&nbsp; y/y Growth = <i>Y<sub>t</sub></i> / <i>Y<sub>t−4</sub></i> − 1',
  'ch4.p2': 'That memory generates the <strong>base effect</strong>: even if current quarterly growth is completely ordinary, a steep plunge four quarters ago produces an optical spike in year-on-year rates today. Even subtler are <strong>seasonal adjustment</strong> and <strong>data revisions</strong>: raw, unadjusted data reliably collapses in Q1 after the Christmas holiday or Lunar New Year; yet when holiday dates shift or habits alter, seasonal filters manufacture false signals. Meanwhile, every "advance estimate" released today is routinely rewritten months and years later.',
  'ch4.model.title': 'Model 4 · Base Effects, 2×4 Centered Moving Average Adjustment & ALFRED Revision History',
  'ch4.model.how': 'Switch between three modes: (1) Base effects (see how Year 3 Q1 y/y spikes on ordinary quarterly growth); (2) Seasonal adjustment (classical 2×4 centered moving average decomposition with a 6% seasonal wave); (3) Real revision history (U.S. 2008Q4, 2020Q2, and 2022H1 advance estimates vs latest 2026 vintages).',
  'ch4.model.aria': 'Charts comparing q/q SAAR vs y/y rates, raw vs seasonally adjusted series, and historical US GDP revision vintages',
