  'ch4.cv.lat': '2026-11 Latest Vintage',
  'ch4.say.rates': 'After a plunge in Quarter 5 (q/q <em>{dip}%</em>, annualized <em>{dipS}%</em>), the economy in Quarter 9 had long returned to its ordinary 0.5% pace (SAAR <em>{st}%</em>), yet y/y growth leaped to <em>{echo}%</em> purely on the low base!',
  'ch4.say.seasonal': 'In raw unadjusted data, every Q1 exhibits a fake crash to <em>{nsa}%</em> SAAR; isolating seasonal factors with a 2×4 moving average recovers the smooth true trend of <em>{sa}%</em>.',
  'ch4.say.shift': 'Note the end of Year 5: shifting merely <em>{sh}%</em> of spending forward from Q4 to Q3 causes fixed seasonal filters to distort Q4 SAAR to <em>{sa5}%</em>.',
  'ch4.say.rev': 'Real history (ALFRED): 2008Q4 was first reported at <em>−3.80%</em>, revised to <em>−8.89%</em> in 2011, and stands at <em>−8.47%</em> today; 2022Q2 read <em>−0.93%</em> in August 2022 (two negative quarters), but has now been upgraded to a positive <em>+0.56%</em>!',
  'ch4.p3': 'In the baseline "base effect" scenario, steady quarterly growth is 0.5% (annualized <strong>2.02%</strong>). Quarter 5 experiences a sudden <strong>−4.0%</strong> contraction (SAAR <strong>−15.07%</strong>, y/y <strong>−2.55%</strong>); after a mild rebound in Quarter 6, growth immediately returns to 0.5% per quarter. Yet four quarters later in Quarter 9, with no new acceleration whatsoever, y/y growth magically surges to <strong>+3.54%</strong>! Under "seasonal adjustment," a 6% holiday amplitude drives raw unadjusted (NSA) Q1 annualized growth down to a staggering <strong>−36.66%</strong>, while the seasonally adjusted (SA) series tracks the true underlying trend of <strong>+2.42%</strong>.',
  'ch4.p4': 'Switching to "Historical Revisions" presents numbers far more striking than synthetic models (drawn from the St. Louis Fed’s ALFRED vintage database): during the height of the 2008 financial crisis in <strong>2008Q4</strong>, the advance estimate in February 2009 was just <strong>−3.80%</strong>, revised to <strong>−6.34%</strong> by the third estimate and plunged to <strong>−8.89%</strong> in the 2011 annual revision (now standing at <strong>−8.47%</strong> as of November 2026)—the advance release missed over half the recession’s severity. Similarly, <strong>2020Q2</strong> was initially reported at −32.90% and subsequently revised to −27.98%. And the fiercely debated 2022 "technical recession" (where August 2022 vintages showed 2022Q1 at <strong>−1.57%</strong> and 2022Q2 at <strong>−0.93%</strong>) has been revised by November 2026 so that 2022Q2 is now positive at <strong>+0.56%</strong>! Long-term BEA studies show average absolute revisions from advance to second estimates are <strong>0.5</strong> percentage points, to third estimates <strong>0.6</strong> percentage points, and to latest benchmark revisions a full <strong>1.3</strong> percentage points.',
  'ch4.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> The seasonal adjustment here uses a classic 2×4 centered moving average for didactic clarity; statistical agencies use X-13ARIMA-SEATS with holiday regressors. Floating holidays such as the Lunar New Year move across roughly three weeks of the Gregorian calendar, creating substantial moving-holiday bias.',

  /* ── ch5: Comparing countries & Inequality ── */
  'ch5.title': 'Cross-Country Comparisons & Distribution: Whose $100, Whose Average?',
  'ch5.law': 'Market exchange-rate GDP understates physical living standards in lower-wage economies because haircuts and housing cannot be shipped overseas; within a nation, when growth accrues to the top, GDP per capita diverges from median income.',
  'ch5.p1': 'According to the IMF World Economic Outlook (October 2026), market-exchange nominal GDP stands at <strong>$32.64 trillion</strong> for the United States, <strong>$21.55 trillion</strong> for China, and <strong>$4.06 trillion</strong> for India. Converted to <strong>Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)</strong>, the U.S. remains $32.64 trillion, while China rises to <strong>$44.55 trillion</strong> and India reaches <strong>$19.19 trillion</strong> (more than quadruple). Why does the exact same economic output in the exact same year look two to four times larger under a different ruler?',
  'ch5.p2': 'The core mechanism is the <strong>Balassa–Samuelson effect</strong>. Divide an economy into two sectors: the <strong>traded goods sector</strong> (cars, microchips, textiles, productivity <i>A<sub>T</sub></i>) subject to global competition, and the <strong>non-traded services sector</strong> (haircuts, restaurant meals, rent, productivity <i>A<sub>N</sub></i>) consumed locally. Traded goods face global competition, tying domestic wages <i>w</i> to <i>A<sub>T</sub></i>. Because local barbers must compete with factories for workers, non-traded prices follow <i>p<sub>N</sub></i> = <i>w</i> / <i>A<sub>N</sub></i> = <i>A<sub>T</sub></i> / <i>A<sub>N</sub></i>. When a developing economy has traded productivity <i>A<sub>T</sub></i> at only 35% of advanced economies, its service prices and aggregate price level <i>P</i> are systematically lower. Meanwhile, even when aggregate GDP is measured accurately, the <strong>mean</strong> frequently masks stagnation in the <strong>median</strong>—even if GDP per capita expands 20%, if growth flows predominantly to top earners, the Lorenz curve bends downward and the Gini coefficient climbs.',
  'ch5.eq': '<i>p<sub>N</sub></i> = <i>A<sub>T</sub></i> / <i>A<sub>N</sub></i> ,&nbsp; <i>P</i> = (<i>A<sub>T</sub></i> / <i>A<sub>N</sub></i>)<sup>1−<i>γ</i></sup> ;&nbsp;&nbsp; Gini Coefficient <i>G</i> = 1 − 2∫<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup> <i>L</i>(<i>p</i>) d<i>p</i>',
  'ch5.model.title': 'Model 5 · Balassa–Samuelson PPP & Lorenz Curve Lab',
  'ch5.model.how': 'Under "Exchange Rate vs PPP," adjust traded and non-traded productivity to see how local prices drive a wedge between exchange-rate and PPP output (benchmarked against 2026 IMF data); under "Lorenz & Gini," adjust distribution skew to see mean and median income diverge.',
  'ch5.model.aria': 'Interactive charts comparing market exchange rate vs PPP output, and Lorenz curves with Gini coefficients',
