  'ch5.say.ppp': 'With traded productivity <i>A<sub>T</sub></i> = <em>{at}</em> and non-traded <i>A<sub>N</sub></i> = <em>{an}</em>, non-traded prices are <em>{pN}</em>× of the benchmark country and overall price level <i>P</i> = <em>{P}</em>—market FX GDP per capita is <em>{fx}</em>, while real PPP consumption reaches <em>{ppp}</em> (<em>{mult}×</em> of market FX).',
  'ch5.say.lorSkew': 'Even with mean income rising <em>{mean}%</em>: because gains accrued heavily to the top, median income rose only <em>{med}%</em> and the Gini coefficient widened from <em>{g0}</em> to <em>{g1}</em>!',
  'ch5.say.lorEven': 'Under proportional growth, mean income rises <em>{mean}%</em> and median income matches it at <em>{med}%</em>; the Lorenz curve remains unchanged and the Gini coefficient holds steady at <em>{g1}</em>.',
  'ch5.p3': 'Benchmarking default parameters against reality: when <i>A<sub>T</sub></i> = 0.35 and <i>A<sub>N</sub></i> = 0.60, non-traded prices are only 0.58× the advanced benchmark and relative price level <i>P</i> = 0.70, yielding PPP per capita output of <strong>49.7</strong>—a <strong>1.42×</strong> increase over the market exchange rate value of <strong>35.0</strong>. This illustrates why market exchange rates measure an economy’s international purchasing power for semiconductors, crude oil, or foreign debt, while PPP reflects the actual domestic standard of living.',
  'ch5.p4': 'Switching to "Lorenz Curve & Median": under default parameters (top 10% holding an initial 35% income share, starting Gini <strong>0.446</strong>), even if per capita GDP grows <strong>20.0%</strong>, when over 90% of gains accrue to the top decile, real median income rises just <strong>4.2%</strong> while Gini climbs to <strong>0.498</strong>. Only when top-skewed growth is turned off does the median match the full <strong>20.0%</strong> gain, leaving Gini unchanged at 0.446.',
  'ch5.see': 'Further reading: On long-term PPP convergence of exchange rates, see <a href="../../money/where-money-comes-from/#ch9">Chapter 9 of “Where Money Comes From”</a>; on why mean and median diverge under fat tails, see <a href="../../../probability/everyday/numbers-lie/#c8">Chapter 8 of “Every Number Was True, and the Conclusion Was Still Wrong”</a> and <a href="../../../probability/statistics/mean-median-mode/">Mean, Median, Mode</a>; on mechanisms of wealth concentration, see <a href="../../../demos/scarcity-moves/#c9">Chapter 9 of “Scarcity Moves”</a>.',
  'ch5.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> Balassa–Samuelson isolates two-sector productivity differentials; real-world tariffs, capital flows, foreign exchange intervention, and property tax regimes also shape price gaps. Furthermore, constructing globally comparable consumption baskets across diverse cultures is intrinsically challenging (e.g. comparing dietary staples or public vs private healthcare).',

  /* ── ch6: Labour market ── */
  'ch6.title': 'Labor Market: Why Falling Unemployment Is Not Always Good News',
  'ch6.law': 'Official U-3 unemployment counts only active job-seekers in the labor force denominator; when the jobless give up searching or full-time workers are reduced to part-time gigs, U-3 cosmetically drops.',
  'ch6.p1': 'Under ILO and U.S. BLS standards, the working-age population is divided into three groups: the <strong>employed (E)</strong>, the <strong>unemployed (U)</strong> without a job who actively sought work in the past four weeks, and those <strong>not in the labor force (N)</strong>. The headline unemployment rate is <strong>U-3 = U / (E + U)</strong>, and the labor force participation rate is <strong>LFPR = (E + U) / Population</strong>. Adding <strong>marginally attached workers (M)</strong> who stopped searching and part-time workers who want full-time work for <strong>economic reasons (PT)</strong> yields the broader measure: <strong>U-6 = (U + M + PT) / (E + U + M)</strong>.',
  'ch6.p2': 'A single unemployment headline cannot reveal labor market tightness without two foundational relationships: the <strong>Beveridge curve</strong>, plotting the unemployment rate against job openings—along which vacancies and unemployment move in opposite directions during typical cycles, while deteriorating matching efficiency shifts the curve outward; and <strong>Okun’s law</strong>, which estimates the empirical slope relating GDP growth to changes in the unemployment rate Δ<i>u</i> via OLS—a slope that has drifted substantially across decades.',
  'ch6.model.title': 'Model 6 · Unemployment Definitions (U-3/U-6), Beveridge Curve & Okun’s Law',
  'ch6.model.how': 'Switch across three views: (1) Definitions lab (adjust discouraged workers and involuntary part-time workers); (2) U.S. Beveridge curve (historical 2001–2026-09 FRED trajectory with matching efficiency slider A); (3) Okun’s law regressions (1960–2026 subsample OLS regressions).',
  'ch6.model.aria': 'Charts of working-age population breakdowns, U-3 vs U-6, US Beveridge curve trajectory, and Okun’s law OLS scatter plots',
