  'ch6.say.defsBase': 'Baseline structure mirrors U.S. October 2026 readings (U-3 = 4.1%, U-6 = 7.9%, LFPR = 61.9%): U-3 is <em>{u3}%</em>, U-6 is <em>{u6}%</em>, and participation is <em>{lfpr}%</em>.',
  'ch6.say.defsDisc': 'With <em>{disc}</em> unemployed individuals giving up their search: employment is completely unchanged (E/Pop remains {epop}%), yet official U-3 "improves" to <em>{u3}%</em>, while U-6 holds at <em>{u6}%</em> and LFPR drops to <em>{lfpr}%</em>!',
  'ch6.say.defsPt': 'Adding <em>{pt}</em> involuntary part-time workers: official U-3 is completely unresponsive at <em>{u3}%</em>, while broad U-6 jumps to <em>{u6}%</em>!',
  'ch6.say.bev': 'Actual U.S. trajectory: pre-pandemic (2019-12) stood at 3.6% / 4.2%; by 2022-03 the curve shifted out to a peak vacancy rate of <em>7.5%</em>; by 2026-09 it retreated almost vertically to <em>4.2%</em> / <em>4.5%</em> without triggering a surge in unemployment.',
  'ch6.say.okun': 'Over the <em>{era}</em> period ({n} quarters): each additional 1 percentage point of GDP growth lowers unemployment by <em>{absb}</em> pp; stabilizing unemployment requires <em>{zg}%</em> growth (<i>R</i>² = <em>{r2}</em>)—with slopes varying by more than 2× across eras.',
  'ch6.p3': 'In the definitions lab, simulate 10 unemployed workers stopping their job search: not a single person found work (employment-to-population holds steady at 59.40%), yet official U-3 improves from <strong>4.04%</strong> to <strong>2.46%</strong>, with the labor force participation rate falling to <strong>60.90%</strong>. Next, add 24 involuntary part-time workers: U-3 does not budge, while broad U-6 jumps to <strong>11.73%</strong>. Actual U.S. data for October 2026 reflects <strong>U-3 = 4.1%</strong>, <strong>U-6 = 7.9%</strong>, and <strong>LFPR = 61.9%</strong> (BLS / FRED).',
  'ch6.p4': 'Examine the warnings from both historical curves: on the <strong>Beveridge curve</strong>, by March 2022 U.S. unemployment was 3.7% while the job vacancy rate hit an all-time high of <strong>7.5%</strong>, shifting the curve far outward; by September 2026 vacancies cooled to <strong>4.5%</strong> while unemployment hovered at <strong>4.2%</strong>, dropping almost vertically along the steep upper segment and disproving claims that softening vacancies would inevitably trigger massive joblessness. On <strong>Okun’s law</strong>, the 1960–2019 period (240 quarters) yielded a slope of <strong>−0.36</strong> (<i>R</i>² = 0.59), whereas 2008–2019 (48 quarters) steepened to <strong>−0.64</strong> (<i>R</i>² = 0.74), and 2020–2026 (26 quarters) reached <strong>−0.85</strong>—treating an empirical regression slope as a physical constant inevitably fails when macroeconomic regimes shift.',
  'ch6.see': 'Further reading: On least-squares fitting and spurious correlation traps, see <a href="../../../probability/statistics/regression-correlation/">Regression & Correlation</a>; on distortions when an indicator becomes a target, see <a href="../../../probability/everyday/numbers-lie/#c9">Chapter 9 of “Every Number Was True, and the Conclusion Was Still Wrong” (Goodhart’s Law)</a>.',
  'ch6.limit': '<strong>Model Limitations:</strong> The matching function <i>M</i> = <i>A</i>√(<i>UV</i>) here is a steady-state pedagogical simplification; other economies such as China publish surveyed urban unemployment (averaging 5.2% over the first three quarters of 2026), whose coverage and sampling frames differ from the U.S. Current Population Survey (CPS).',

  /* ── ch7: Business cycle, potential output & Sahm rule ── */
  'ch7.title': 'Business Cycles: Who Calls a Recession, and Why the Ruler Itself Is Rewritten',
  'ch7.law': 'Judging whether an economy is overheating or in recession relies not on a visible real-time position, but on unobservable "potential output" and lagged institutional calls that are repeatedly revised.',
  'ch7.p1': 'Many cite "two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth" as the definition of a recession, but the official arbiter in the United States, the <strong>NBER Business Cycle Dating Committee</strong>, rejects that shorthand rule of thumb. Instead, it reviews monthly real personal income less transfers, nonfarm payrolls, industrial production, and real wholesale/retail sales, typically declaring peaks and troughs 6 to 21 months after they occur. In the summer of 2022, vintages available in August 2022 indeed reported consecutive declines for 2022Q1 and 2022Q2 (−1.57% and −0.93%), yet the NBER never designated a recession—and later revisions in 2026 revised 2022Q2 into positive territory at +0.56%.',
  'ch7.p2': 'To avoid waiting a year for retrospective NBER announcements, former Federal Reserve economist Claudia Sahm designed a real-time empirical indicator based purely on monthly unemployment—the <strong>Sahm Rule</strong>: when the 3-month moving average of the national unemployment rate exceeds the minimum of the 3-month moving averages from the prior 12 months by <strong>0.50 percentage points or more</strong>, a recession is signaled. Another central gauge is <strong>potential output (<i>Y</i>*)</strong> and the resulting <strong>output gap (<i>Y</i>/<i>Y</i>* − 1)</strong> estimated by the Congressional Budget Office (CBO)—yet <i>Y</i>* is unobservable, rewritten whenever population, immigration, or trend productivity estimates are updated.',
  'ch7.eq': 'Sahm<i><sub>t</sub></i> = <i>ū</i><sub>3m</sub>(<i>t</i>) − min<sub><i>k</i>=1..12</sub> <i>ū</i><sub>3m</sub>(<i>t</i> − <i>k</i>) ≥ 0.50 pp ;&nbsp;&nbsp; Output Gap = (<i>Y</i> − <i>Y</i>*) / <i>Y</i>*',
  'ch7.model.title': 'Model 7 · Real-Time Sahm Rule Calculator & CBO Potential Output Revisions',
  'ch7.model.how': 'In "Sahm Rule" mode, sweep the threshold slider across all monthly U.S. unemployment data from 1960 to October 2026 (gray shading marks NBER recessions), and toggle "Focus 2018–2026" to examine the July 2024 trigger; switch to "Potential Output" to see how the 2019 baseline was lifted seven years later.',
  'ch7.model.aria': 'Charts of the U.S. Sahm Rule indicator against NBER recession shading from 1960 to 2026 alongside CBO potential output vintage revisions',
  'ch7.ctl.mode': 'Cycle Diagnostic Tool',
